#nexus [SplitsTree6]

BEGIN SPLITSTREE6;
DIMENSIONS nDataNodes=8 nAlgorithms=6;
PROGRAM version='SplitsTree (version 1.0.0-SNAPSHOT) - License GPL v3';
WORKFLOW creationDate='1793974725274'; [Fri Nov 06 15:18:45 CET 2026]
END; [SPLITSTREE6]

[!20 random points on an integer grid and their Manhattan distances (examples/manhattan_distances_20x20-points.tsv
of the RazorNet repository); RazorNet recovers the grid, shown without simplification (Shave filter target None).
Supplementary Figure S6 of the RazorNet paper.]

[
Methods:
Analysis was performed using RazorNet (version dev)  (Huson and Bryant 2024 ; DH Huson and D. Bryant, The SplitsTree App: interactive analysis and visualization using phylogenetic trees and networks. Nature Methods 21(10):1773-1774 2024.).
The original input consisted of 20 taxa and a 20 x 20 distance matrix.
The Razor Net method was used (default options) so as to obtain 44 nodes and 61 edges.
The Shave Filter method was used (default options, except Target=None) so as to obtain 44 nodes and 61 edges.

References:
Huson and Bryant 2024: DH Huson and D. Bryant, The SplitsTree App: interactive analysis and visualization using phylogenetic trees and networks. Nature Methods 21(10):1773-1774 2024.
]

